%S A252864 1,1,2,3,5,8,12,18,25,35,51,75,110,161,236,346,507,743,1089,1596,2339,
%T A252864 3428,5024,7363,10791,15815,23178,33969,49784,72962,106931,156715,
%U A252864 229677,336608,493323,723000,1059608,1552931,2275931,3335539,4888470,7164401,10499940
%N A252864 Number of pairs in generation n of the tree T defined in Comments.
%C A252864 Generation g(0) of T is (0,0). Thereafter, successive generations accrue according to the rule that if (j,k) is in T, then (j,k+1) and (k,j+k) are in T. An equivalent tree is generated as follows: start with the tree of polynomials, T*, having g(0) = 0 and rule that if p(x) is in T*, then p(x) + 1 and x*p(x) are in T*; then put x = (1+sqrt(5))/2, the golden ratio, and remove duplicates as they occur. Or, to obtain a third guise for T, in T* replace x^2 by x + 1 in every polynomial (e.g., replace x^3 by 2x+1, etc.), and remove duplicates as they occur.
%C A252864 Every ordered pair of nonnegative integers occurs exactly once in T.
%C A252864 Proof of the recurrence. Write the pairs as (a,b) with (j,k) = (a, a+b), so that the two rules of T become A: (a,b) -> (a,b+1) and B: (a,b) -> (a+b,a), acting on all of Z_{>=0}^2; the constraint j <= k becomes b >= 0. Let phi = (1+sqrt(5))/2 and z(a,b) = a*phi + b; then z(A(v)) = z(v) + 1 and z(B(v)) = phi*z(v), and z is injective on Z_{>=0}^2 since phi is irrational. Let l(a,b) be the generation index of (a,b), i.e. the least number of steps from (0,0). Key lemma (bridge): l(a,b) = min { deg(d) + Sum_i d_i }, the minimum being over all representations Sum_i d_i*phi^i = a*phi + b with nonnegative integer digits d_i of finite support, deg(d) = max{i : d_i > 0}. The digits are not bounded above and internal zeros are allowed; no Zeckendorf-type condition is imposed. The minimum is attained, since the cost is a nonnegative integer. The correspondence is explicit: a word A^{d_m} B A^{d_{m-1}} B ... B A^{d_0} has length deg(d) + Sum_i d_i, and a shortest word never begins with B, because B fixes (0,0) and deleting a leading B gives a strictly shorter word with the same value. From the lemma, a five-state decomposition of the nodes with a >= 8 and b >= 1, together with an explicit invariant of its transition matrix, yields a(n) = a(n-1) + a(n-3) for n >= 13; direct computation verifies n = 12. This settles for phi the case left open in Ballot, Kimberling and Moses (2017), where the analogous statement is proved for r = sqrt(d) with d not a square; the case r = sqrt(2) was proved by M. Stoll (MathOverflow 195207), and the method here is the phi-analog of Lemma 4.1 of that paper, the difference being that expansions in powers of phi with nonnegative integer digits are not unique, so the length is a minimum over expansions rather than a function of a canonical one. - _Rafał Wiktorowicz_, Aug 24 2026
%H A252864 Christian Ballot, Clark Kimberling, and Peter J. C. Moses, <a href="https://www.fq.math.ca/Papers1/55-5/BallotKimberlingMoses.pdf">Linear Recurrences Originating From Polynomial Trees</a>, Fibonacci Quart. 55 (2017), no. 5, 15-27.
%H A252864 M. Stoll, <a href="https://mathoverflow.net/questions/195207">A possibly surprising appearance of Lucas numbers</a>, MathOverflow, 2015. [Proof of the sqrt(2) case; the conjecture for phi is stated there.]
%H A252864 Rafał Wiktorowicz, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22092441">Formal proof in Lean 4</a>, Zenodo, 2026.
%F A252864 Conjecture: |g(n)| = |g(n-1)| + |g(n-3)| for n >= 12.
%F A252864 Empirical g.f.: (x-1)*(x^2+x+1)*(x^8+2*x^7+2*x^6+2*x^5+x^4+x^3+x^2+1) / (x^3+x-1). - _Colin Barker_, Feb 01 2015
%F A252864 Theorem: |g(n)| = |g(n-1)| + |g(n-3)| for n >= 12. - _Rafał Wiktorowicz_, Aug 14 Since the recurrence is proved, the g.f. above is no longer empirical: (1-x-x^3)*A(x) equals minus Barker's numerator, a polynomial of degree 11. - _Rafał Wiktorowicz_, Aug 24 2026
%e A252864 Ordered pairs (i,j) are abbreviated as i,j in this list of 7 generations of T:
%e A252864   g(0):  0,0
%e A252864   g(1):  0,1
%e A252864   g(2):  0,2  1,1
%e A252864   g(3):  0,3  1,2  2,2
%e A252864   g(4):  0,4  1,3  2,3  2,4  3,3
%e A252864   g(5):  0,5  1,4  2,5  3,4  3,5  3,6  4,4  4,6
%e A252864   g(6):  0,6  1,5  2,6  3,7  4,5  4,7  4,8  5,5  5,7  5,8  6,9  6,10
%t A252864 t = NestList[DeleteDuplicates[Flatten[Map[{# + {0, 1}, {Last[#], Total[#]}} &, #], 1]] &, {{0, 0}}, 30]; s[0] = t[[1]]; s[n_] := s[n] = Union[t[[n + 1]], s[n - 1]];
%t A252864 g[n_] := Complement[s[n], s[n - 1]]; g[0] = {{0, 0}};
%t A252864 Column[Table[g[z], {z, 0, 9}]]
%t A252864 Table[Length[g[z]], {z, 0, 10}]
